      "completion_tokens": 1165,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance with the chemical formula **H₂O**. This means that each molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\nWater is a **polar molecule**. Because oxygen is more \"thirsty\" for electrons than hydrogen, the oxygen atom pulls the shared electrons closer to itself. This creates a slight negative charge on the oxygen side and a slight positive charge on the hydrogen side. This polarity is what allows water to stick to itself (cohesion) and to other substances (adhesion).\n\n### 2. The Three States of Matter\nWater is unique because it is the only common substance found naturally on Earth in all three physical states:\n*   **Solid:** Ice (frozen water).\n*   **Liquid:** The state we drink and use for washing.\n*   **Gas:** Water vapor (invisible gas in the air).\n\n### 3. Unique Physical Properties\nWater has several properties that make it essential for life:\n*   **Universal Solvent:** Because of its polarity, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This allows it to transport nutrients and minerals inside living organisms and helps regulate the chemistry of the Earth.\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Water molecules stick together very strongly at the surface, creating a kind of \"skin.\" This allows small insects to walk across ponds without sinking.\n*   **High Specific Heat:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before it gets hot, and it releases a lot of heat before it freezes. This helps regulate the Earth's climate and keeps the temperature of our bodies stable.\n\n### 4. Biological Importance\nWater is the **foundation of life**. It is the primary component of all known forms of life. It is used by cells for:\n*   Transporting nutrients and waste.\n*   Chemical reactions (digestion, photosynthesis).\n*   Lubricating joints and tissues.\n*   Regulating body temperature through sweating and panting.\n\nIn summary, water is a simple molecule (H₂O) that acts as a universal solvent and a vital medium for life, capable of existing as a solid, liquid, and gas."
